1
00:00:01,718 --> 00:00:05,603
Imagine you're a child in an
Alpine village, a century ago.

2
00:00:05,653 --> 00:00:09,190
It's the fifth of December,
it's already dark,

3
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:12,270
and somewhere down the
lane you can hear bells…

4
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:13,977
and then chains.

5
00:00:14,027 --> 00:00:18,750
There's a knock at the door,
and an old bishop steps inside.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:24,580
And behind him, half hidden in the
doorway, something black and hairy is

7
00:00:24,630 --> 00:00:28,810
waiting, with horns, a long red tongue…

8
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:31,890
and a basket on its back.

9
00:00:32,364 --> 00:00:34,170
So who is this creature?

10
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,630
Why does a saint keep a devil on a chain?

11
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:41,050
And why did a government
once try to get rid of him?

12
00:00:42,513 --> 00:00:45,180
Welcome to the Untold Legend Archive.

13
00:00:45,230 --> 00:00:47,500
Today's story is…

14
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:49,053
Krampus.

15
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:56,490
Now, if you know Krampus from
the 2015 Hollywood film,

16
00:00:56,540 --> 00:01:00,403
you probably picture a
sort of evil anti-Santa,

17
00:01:00,453 --> 00:01:03,490
dragging whole families off into the snow.

18
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:07,358
And honestly, when I started
digging into him for this video,

19
00:01:07,408 --> 00:01:09,870
that's roughly what I expected to find.

20
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:13,390
A pagan monster at war with Christmas.

21
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:16,057
It turns out it's the other way round.

22
00:01:16,107 --> 00:01:20,130
Krampus works for the man who
would one day become Santa Claus.

23
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:23,370
And he takes the job very seriously.

24
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:28,234
If you want to see what that looks
like, the Gastein valley near Salzburg

25
00:01:28,284 --> 00:01:32,214
is a good place to start, because the
visit there still happens much the

26
00:01:32,264 --> 00:01:34,821
way it probably did a century ago.

27
00:01:34,870 --> 00:01:39,986
On the fifth and sixth of December,
Saint Nicholas goes from farmhouse to

28
00:01:40,036 --> 00:01:45,014
farmhouse, with an angel at his side
and a whole pack of Krampuses behind

29
00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:48,714
him. The door opens, and the room changes.

30
00:01:48,764 --> 00:01:52,354
Bells crash against the
walls, chairs go over,

31
00:01:52,404 --> 00:01:56,220
and a Krampus might grab the father
of the house and wrestle him to the

32
00:01:56,270 --> 00:02:00,618
floor, while the children squeeze
themselves into the corner of the bench.

33
00:02:00,668 --> 00:02:04,674
Then the bishop raises a
hand, everyone goes quiet,

34
00:02:04,724 --> 00:02:06,294
and the questions begin.

35
00:02:07,022 --> 00:02:08,321
Have you been good?

36
00:02:08,371 --> 00:02:09,908
Do you know your lessons?

37
00:02:09,958 --> 00:02:11,521
Can you say your prayers?

38
00:02:11,571 --> 00:02:16,268
In the eighteenth and nineteenth
centuries, this was basically an exam,

39
00:02:16,318 --> 00:02:19,088
held in real homes and real schools.

40
00:02:19,138 --> 00:02:23,158
And everyone in the room could see
exactly who was waiting for the wrong

41
00:02:23,208 --> 00:02:24,208
answers.

42
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,030
His tools were pretty simple.

43
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:32,030
A bundle of birch twigs for beating,
chains that rattled as he moved,

44
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:34,710
and a basket or a sack on his back.

45
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:40,057
In some of the older tales, a child who
went into that sack might be drowned,

46
00:02:40,107 --> 00:02:43,163
eaten, or carried straight down to Hell.

47
00:02:43,213 --> 00:02:47,414
In the end, of course, the sweets come
out, the Krampuses move on to the

48
00:02:47,464 --> 00:02:50,290
next house, and everyone breathes again.

49
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:54,210
But I don't think anyone forgets
the sound of those bells.

50
00:02:54,744 --> 00:02:57,386
He even followed you into the new year.

51
00:02:57,437 --> 00:03:02,378
In Styria, a family might be given a
bundle of birch with the twigs painted

52
00:03:02,428 --> 00:03:06,466
gold, and it would hang in the
house all year as a reminder.

53
00:03:06,516 --> 00:03:09,306
A small, shiny threat on the wall.

54
00:03:09,356 --> 00:03:14,706
And in Croatia, a good child would find
a golden branch next to their presents,

55
00:03:14,757 --> 00:03:17,406
but a naughty one would
find the presents gone,

56
00:03:17,456 --> 00:03:19,946
and a silver branch left in their place.

57
00:03:19,997 --> 00:03:23,446
Which feels like a very
polite way of being robbed.

58
00:03:24,022 --> 00:03:28,748
Even his name sounds like a threat,
although we can't be completely sure

59
00:03:28,798 --> 00:03:30,175
where it comes from.

60
00:03:30,225 --> 00:03:33,798
It's usually linked to the
old German word Krampe,

61
00:03:33,848 --> 00:03:35,908
meaning a hook or a claw.

62
00:03:35,958 --> 00:03:40,015
The other explanation points
to a Bavarian dialect word,

63
00:03:40,065 --> 00:03:43,481
Krampn, for something
dried out, shrivelled…

64
00:03:43,531 --> 00:03:45,368
or dead. Lovely.

65
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,430
So where did he actually come from?

66
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:51,790
Well, to answer that, we have
to start with the bishop.

67
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,010
Because Saint Nicholas has a
few dark stories of his own.

68
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:02,377
Three boys are on their way to school.

69
00:04:02,427 --> 00:04:07,050
It's getting dark, they're cold, so
they stop at an inn for the night.

70
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,897
The innkeeper seems friendly enough.

71
00:04:09,947 --> 00:04:12,810
He gives them a meal and a bed.

72
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:16,850
But he's also noticed the
money they're carrying.

73
00:04:17,492 --> 00:04:22,402
That night, while the boys are
asleep, he and his wife kill them.

74
00:04:22,452 --> 00:04:26,994
They cut them into pieces, and
pack them into a barrel of salt,

75
00:04:27,044 --> 00:04:28,974
to be sold as pork.

76
00:04:30,337 --> 00:04:34,574
Three boys, whose only mistake
was knocking on the wrong door.

77
00:04:35,258 --> 00:04:41,059
Some time later, a bishop turns up at
the same inn and asks for something

78
00:04:41,109 --> 00:04:44,205
to eat. And not just anything.

79
00:04:44,255 --> 00:04:47,032
He wants whatever's in the barrel.

80
00:04:47,082 --> 00:04:51,392
As you can probably guess,
the innkeeper isn't thrilled.

81
00:04:51,442 --> 00:04:56,592
The bishop walks over, makes the
sign of the cross over the barrel…

82
00:04:56,642 --> 00:05:01,412
and one by one, the three
boys climb out, alive.

83
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:07,490
The bishop, of course, is Nicholas,
and this is the story that made him

84
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,610
the patron saint of children.

85
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:15,050
Now, historians are pretty clear that
it has no historical value at all.

86
00:05:15,147 --> 00:05:18,550
It only seems to appear
in the late Middle Ages.

87
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:24,163
But it spread everywhere, and for
centuries painters showed Nicholas with

88
00:05:24,213 --> 00:05:29,190
three little children standing in a
tub, which is a slightly unsettling

89
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:31,050
thing to put on a church wall.

90
00:05:32,355 --> 00:05:37,142
The real Nicholas, as far as we
can tell, was a bishop of Myra,

91
00:05:37,192 --> 00:05:41,015
on the southern coast of what's
now Turkey, in the fourth century.

92
00:05:41,065 --> 00:05:43,656
And we know very little about him.

93
00:05:43,725 --> 00:05:48,622
The oldest full account of his life
was written about five hundred years

94
00:05:48,672 --> 00:05:53,975
after he died, and its most famous
story is a much gentler one.

95
00:05:54,325 --> 00:05:58,935
A poor man has three daughters
and no money for their dowries.

96
00:05:59,085 --> 00:06:04,535
So at night, in secret, Nicholas
throws gold through his window.

97
00:06:04,625 --> 00:06:09,975
And that's really the seed of the
gift-giver who comes while you sleep.

98
00:06:11,275 --> 00:06:16,015
But in eastern France, the
barrel story got one more twist.

99
00:06:16,065 --> 00:06:21,102
The murderous innkeeper repents,
or in some versions is forced,

100
00:06:21,152 --> 00:06:23,395
to become the saint's servant.

101
00:06:23,445 --> 00:06:28,605
He's known as Père Fouettard,
which means Father Whipper,

102
00:06:28,655 --> 00:06:32,735
and he still follows Nicholas on
his rounds, carrying the whip.

103
00:06:32,965 --> 00:06:38,315
So, just to be clear, the man who
butchered three children ends up on the

104
00:06:38,365 --> 00:06:39,935
Christmas staff.

105
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:44,490
So from very early on, the
gift-giver had a shadow.

106
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:48,870
But in the Alps, that shadow
didn't come from the Church.

107
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:52,210
It came out of the forest, wearing a mask.

108
00:06:56,107 --> 00:06:59,403
There's a story from the Rauris
valley, south of Salzburg,

109
00:06:59,453 --> 00:07:01,030
about a Perchten run.

110
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:06,590
The Perchten were masked figures,
some beautiful and some hideous,

111
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:10,290
who danced through the villages
in the dark weeks of winter.

112
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:15,542
And on this particular night, the
ugly ones, dressed as devils,

113
00:07:15,592 --> 00:07:18,210
were dancing in front of a farmhouse.

114
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:24,697
Before the dance began, their leader
did something he really shouldn't have.

115
00:07:24,747 --> 00:07:28,630
He took off the blessed scapular
he wore around his neck,

116
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:31,902
a little holy image that
was meant to protect him.

117
00:07:31,952 --> 00:07:36,163
Then the music started,
and he began to change.

118
00:07:36,213 --> 00:07:41,810
He leapt higher than any man could
jump, he spun faster and faster,

119
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:44,542
and when the villagers
looked down at his feet,

120
00:07:44,592 --> 00:07:46,430
they weren't feet any more.

121
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:49,130
They were goat's hooves.

122
00:07:50,242 --> 00:07:54,448
Someone sprinkled him with holy
water, and it did nothing.

123
00:07:54,571 --> 00:08:00,028
So, according to the story, they
fetched the village fire pump and hosed

124
00:08:00,078 --> 00:08:02,334
him down with holy water instead.

125
00:08:02,384 --> 00:08:07,008
Which, I have to admit, is not where
I thought this story was going.

126
00:08:07,058 --> 00:08:08,894
But it worked.

127
00:08:08,944 --> 00:08:13,540
He dropped to the ground as if he were
dead, and he only came round when

128
00:08:13,590 --> 00:08:16,954
they hung the scapular
back around his neck.

129
00:08:17,836 --> 00:08:21,206
The Gastein valley has
a much darker version.

130
00:08:21,276 --> 00:08:26,226
A miner hears an old woman say that
the Perchten runs would be far more

131
00:08:26,276 --> 00:08:30,133
fun if you skipped your prayers
for two weeks beforehand.

132
00:08:30,184 --> 00:08:31,726
So he tries it.

133
00:08:31,776 --> 00:08:35,920
And at the next run, his mask
isn't just a mask any more.

134
00:08:35,969 --> 00:08:41,080
He jumps onto a well, then onto
a roof, and then he flies,

135
00:08:41,130 --> 00:08:43,507
right out over the pine forest.

136
00:08:43,556 --> 00:08:49,107
The priest brings out the Blessed
Sacrament and blesses the four directions,

137
00:08:49,156 --> 00:08:52,634
and the figure falls out
of the sky, screaming.

138
00:08:52,684 --> 00:08:56,747
When the villagers reach him,
they recognise the miner.

139
00:08:56,796 --> 00:09:01,086
He tells them the devil left him
the moment the priest arrived.

140
00:09:01,136 --> 00:09:03,766
And then he dies.

141
00:09:04,796 --> 00:09:09,450
What I find interesting is that these
stories all seem to share the same

142
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:14,354
rule. Behind the mask, you
were never quite yourself,

143
00:09:14,404 --> 00:09:17,354
and something else might
be waiting to step in.

144
00:09:17,404 --> 00:09:21,857
There was even a tradition that anyone
killed while wearing a devil's mask

145
00:09:21,907 --> 00:09:24,534
couldn't be buried in a churchyard.

146
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:28,990
The authorities weren't
keen on any of this either.

147
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:34,457
In 1730, the Archbishop of Salzburg
banned young men from running around

148
00:09:34,507 --> 00:09:37,070
in costume at Epiphany and Carnival.

149
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:43,070
And there's an army record from
1763 of a man who was conscripted,

150
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:46,950
as a punishment, for illegal
Perchten running at night.

151
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,790
But every winter, the
masks came out anyway.

152
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:56,330
And the most frightening figure
behind them wasn't a devil at all.

153
00:10:00,210 --> 00:10:02,650
Her name was Perchta.

154
00:10:03,744 --> 00:10:07,926
We find her in the records from
at least the thirteenth century,

155
00:10:07,976 --> 00:10:12,806
and she walked during the twelve dark
nights between Christmas and Epiphany.

156
00:10:12,856 --> 00:10:14,897
She was checking two things.

157
00:10:14,946 --> 00:10:16,656
Had the spinning been done?

158
00:10:16,706 --> 00:10:19,246
And had you kept the
fast on her feast night?

159
00:10:19,896 --> 00:10:24,930
So picture a girl who sneaks a proper
meal on the last night of the holidays,

160
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:26,714
when she was supposed to be fasting.

161
00:10:26,764 --> 00:10:29,434
She goes to bed full and warm.

162
00:10:29,484 --> 00:10:33,054
And sometime after
midnight, the door opens.

163
00:10:33,964 --> 00:10:36,494
According to the folklore
Jacob Grimm collected,

164
00:10:36,544 --> 00:10:40,314
Perchta would cut open the belly
of anyone who broke the fast,

165
00:10:40,364 --> 00:10:42,066
and stuff it with straw.

166
00:10:42,116 --> 00:10:46,594
Then she'd sew it back up, using a
ploughshare for a needle and an iron

167
00:10:46,644 --> 00:10:48,014
chain for thread.

168
00:10:48,580 --> 00:10:52,534
Next to that, a few strokes of
the birch sound like a holiday.

169
00:10:53,060 --> 00:10:55,630
But Perchta could also reward you.

170
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:59,750
A hard-working spinner might wake up
to find her spindle full of golden

171
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:05,250
thread. And she had a gentler side too,
one that far fewer people know about.

172
00:11:06,347 --> 00:11:10,563
In the Orla valley, in central Germany,
she was remembered as the queen

173
00:11:10,613 --> 00:11:11,857
of the Heimchen.

174
00:11:11,907 --> 00:11:15,990
These were tiny figures dressed in
white, and the stories say they were

175
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:19,902
the souls of children who had died
before they could be baptised.

176
00:11:19,952 --> 00:11:22,110
They lived alongside the farmers.

177
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:26,550
In the evenings they danced in the
meadows, and at night they quietly helped

178
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:27,670
out in the fields.

179
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:31,370
And for as long as they
stayed, the valley grew rich.

180
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:34,897
Then one day a stranger arrived.

181
00:11:34,947 --> 00:11:38,890
He warned the villagers that
Perchta couldn't be trusted,

182
00:11:38,940 --> 00:11:43,252
and that the little helpers in their
fields were the souls of unbaptised

183
00:11:43,302 --> 00:11:46,110
children. So the people
turned away from them.

184
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:50,490
And Perchta gathered up her Heimchen,
led them down to the river…

185
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:51,690
and across it…

186
00:11:51,740 --> 00:11:53,170
and they never came back.

187
00:11:53,782 --> 00:11:58,130
Of everything I read for this video,
that's the moment that stays with me.

188
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:02,550
Now, the Perchten, the people
who dressed up as her followers,

189
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:06,090
may well have run through the same
valleys as the devils of the Church.

190
00:12:06,140 --> 00:12:10,610
And according to the writer Al
Ridenour, when the bans failed,

191
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:12,163
the custom simply bent.

192
00:12:12,213 --> 00:12:15,190
Saint Nicholas was brought
into the procession,

193
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:18,170
and the wild masked figure
became his servant.

194
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:21,017
Ridenour actually has a
great way of putting it.

195
00:12:21,067 --> 00:12:25,690
He calls Perchta the pagan
Krampus, without Saint Nicholas.

196
00:12:29,716 --> 00:12:34,410
Which brings us to the most popular
story about where Krampus came from.

197
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:40,870
If you look Krampus up online, you'll
find the same claim again and again.

198
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:43,297
He's older than Christianity.

199
00:12:43,347 --> 00:12:45,610
He's a horned pagan god.

200
00:12:45,660 --> 00:12:50,350
And according to quite a few
articles, he's the son of Hel,

201
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:52,770
the Norse ruler of the dead.

202
00:12:53,384 --> 00:12:56,714
It's a great story, and I
can see why people love it.

203
00:12:56,764 --> 00:12:59,546
Part of me wanted it to be true as well.

204
00:12:59,596 --> 00:13:03,446
The trouble is, there's
almost nothing behind it.

205
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:08,130
In the Norse sources,
Hel rules over the dead,

206
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:11,030
and Krampus doesn't appear in any of them.

207
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:14,270
Old Norse was never spoken in the Alps.

208
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:19,398
And the oldest evidence we have for
Krampus himself only goes back to the

209
00:13:19,448 --> 00:13:21,350
late sixteenth century.

210
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:26,370
The Austrian anthropologist
Matthäus Rest puts it quite simply:

211
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:31,411
a pre-Christian origin is
possible, but highly unlikely.

212
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:36,810
He even suspects the word Krampus
is a fairly late invention.

213
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:41,078
And Ridenour traces the
Norse link, at least partly,

214
00:13:41,128 --> 00:13:43,430
back to a modern fantasy novel.

215
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:47,390
So if you've read that
Krampus is Loki's grandson,

216
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,930
I'm afraid that's basically fan fiction.

217
00:13:51,780 --> 00:13:55,750
The family tree we can
actually trace is less exotic,

218
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:58,003
but I think it's more interesting.

219
00:13:58,053 --> 00:14:01,897
In medieval Europe, the
Devil was everywhere.

220
00:14:01,947 --> 00:14:07,540
He was painted on church walls, carved
over doorways and doodled in the

221
00:14:07,590 --> 00:14:12,670
margins of books, usually
with horns, claws, hooves,

222
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:18,270
black hairy skin, and very
often a long, lolling tongue.

223
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:23,890
If you put a medieval devil next
to a Krampus postcard from 1900,

224
00:14:23,940 --> 00:14:27,370
the family resemblance
is pretty hard to miss.

225
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:31,863
The Devil was also a bit of a stage star.

226
00:14:31,913 --> 00:14:36,470
Plays about Saint Nicholas survive in
German manuscripts from the eleventh

227
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:41,480
and twelfth centuries, and Nicholas
was the perfect hero for them,

228
00:14:41,530 --> 00:14:46,881
because one early account of his life
has him driving demons out of a pagan

229
00:14:46,931 --> 00:14:52,417
temple. Then, by 1506, a stage
record from the German town

230
00:14:52,467 --> 00:14:59,160
of Zerbst lists Saint Nicholas and Saint
Michael leading a devil in chains.

231
00:14:59,210 --> 00:15:01,003
And there's the chain.

232
00:15:01,053 --> 00:15:03,690
It's still on Krampus today.

233
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:09,047
Around the seventeenth century,
the roles seem to have settled.

234
00:15:09,097 --> 00:15:12,963
People felt a saint shouldn't
be doing the beating himself,

235
00:15:13,013 --> 00:15:15,390
so the punishment went to a helper.

236
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:19,683
In parts of Germany, that
was Knecht Ruprecht.

237
00:15:19,733 --> 00:15:23,060
In France, it was Père Fouettard.

238
00:15:23,110 --> 00:15:26,243
And in the Alps, it was Krampus.

239
00:15:26,293 --> 00:15:32,123
In the folk plays, Krampus made fun
of quack doctors and just about

240
00:15:32,173 --> 00:15:37,310
every trade in the village, and a lot
of the plays ended with the devils

241
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:41,283
leaping off the stage and
charging through the audience.

242
00:15:41,333 --> 00:15:45,210
Which is one way to make
sure nobody leaves early.

243
00:15:49,170 --> 00:15:53,990
For centuries he stayed a mountain
figure, and what made him famous was

244
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:56,170
something surprisingly ordinary.

245
00:15:56,220 --> 00:15:57,850
The post.

246
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:04,670
From around 1885 until the First
World War, printers churned

247
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:08,550
out hundreds of cheap, brightly
coloured Krampus cards.

248
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:12,830
Most of them carried the
greeting "Gruß vom Krampus",

249
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:18,379
greetings from Krampus, and they were
sent all over the Austro-Hungarian

250
00:16:18,429 --> 00:16:24,470
Empire. Rest compares them to a
kind of nineteenth-century meme,

251
00:16:24,520 --> 00:16:26,610
which I think is spot on.

252
00:16:27,636 --> 00:16:31,174
Some of them show him stuffing
children into his basket.

253
00:16:31,224 --> 00:16:34,974
But a surprising number show
something else entirely.

254
00:16:35,044 --> 00:16:39,514
Krampus chasing young
women, Krampus flirting,

255
00:16:39,564 --> 00:16:44,647
and sometimes Krampus as a woman
herself, leading men astray.

256
00:16:44,697 --> 00:16:47,506
These really weren't for children.

257
00:16:47,556 --> 00:16:50,514
They were more like a
cheeky Valentine's card…

258
00:16:50,564 --> 00:16:52,734
with horns.

259
00:16:53,696 --> 00:16:58,580
In Vienna, sweet shops handed out a
little Krampus devil with every box

260
00:16:58,630 --> 00:17:04,133
of sweets. And when war came
in 1914, he was drafted too.

261
00:17:04,184 --> 00:17:09,786
One card from 1915 shows him
promising to fetch all the Serbs at

262
00:17:09,835 --> 00:17:13,334
once. By then he was everywhere.

263
00:17:13,383 --> 00:17:17,254
And for some people, that
was exactly the problem.

264
00:17:21,133 --> 00:17:26,370
In December 1934, the New York
Times ran a short headline:

265
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:30,290
"Krampus Disliked in Fascist Austria."

266
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:35,350
The country's authoritarian
government, the Fatherland Front,

267
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:38,530
saw itself as the guardian
of Catholic values,

268
00:17:38,580 --> 00:17:42,930
and according to that report,
it moved to ban the custom.

269
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:48,330
A year later, another report from
Vienna said that Krampus was on his way

270
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:53,610
out, and that psychiatrists were worried
about what he was doing to children.

271
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:58,570
And after the Second World
War, the campaign carried on.

272
00:17:58,667 --> 00:18:04,398
In 1953, Time magazine quoted
an Austrian school official

273
00:18:04,448 --> 00:18:09,830
with a very simple plan: let's
begin by throwing out Krampus.

274
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:15,790
The government even handed out pamphlets
with the title Krampus Is an Evil

275
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:19,850
Man. Which, to be fair,
was never really in doubt.

276
00:18:20,538 --> 00:18:26,056
Then in 2006, the city of
Vienna made sure its public

277
00:18:26,106 --> 00:18:32,212
kindergartens stayed free of visits
from both Saint Nicholas and Krampus.

278
00:18:32,262 --> 00:18:35,842
A city councillor said that
both of them create fear,

279
00:18:35,892 --> 00:18:37,612
and have no place there.

280
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:39,977
It didn't work.

281
00:18:40,027 --> 00:18:41,690
It never does.

282
00:18:45,647 --> 00:18:48,590
These days, becoming Krampus
starts with the mask.

283
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:53,646
Around Salzburg, a lot of them are
still carved by hand from Swiss stone

284
00:18:53,696 --> 00:18:57,118
pine. The carver roughs out
the face with a chainsaw,

285
00:18:57,168 --> 00:19:01,470
then shapes the brow, the teeth and
the warts with a knife and a wooden

286
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:07,406
mallet. The eyes go in, the horns go
on, and in the Gastein valley a single

287
00:19:07,456 --> 00:19:11,210
mask can carry six, eight,
or even ten of them.

288
00:19:11,260 --> 00:19:16,630
Add a suit of goat or sheep fur, a
belt of heavy cowbells and a switch,

289
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:19,611
and the man underneath just disappears.

290
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:22,576
And the runs have become enormous.

291
00:19:22,626 --> 00:19:26,602
In Klagenfurt, tens of thousands
of people turn up to watch.

292
00:19:26,652 --> 00:19:28,800
It isn't always harmless, though.

293
00:19:28,850 --> 00:19:32,522
The switches are real, and
some spectators hit back.

294
00:19:32,572 --> 00:19:37,882
In 2013, after several runs in East
Tyrol, eight people ended up in hospital

295
00:19:37,932 --> 00:19:42,600
in Lienz, most of them with broken
bones, and more than sixty others were

296
00:19:42,650 --> 00:19:44,289
treated and sent home.

297
00:19:44,978 --> 00:19:47,268
Then Krampus crossed the Atlantic.

298
00:19:47,318 --> 00:19:51,392
Austrian-Americans were holding a
Krampus Ball in Washington as early as

299
00:19:51,442 --> 00:19:57,352
1962. In the early 2010s, the first
Krampus runs appeared in Los Angeles

300
00:19:57,402 --> 00:20:02,372
and Philadelphia, and in 2015, the
film I mentioned at the start topped

301
00:20:02,422 --> 00:20:04,572
the box office on its opening day.

302
00:20:05,165 --> 00:20:07,372
But something changed along the way.

303
00:20:07,422 --> 00:20:10,732
In the old stories, Krampus
serves Saint Nicholas.

304
00:20:10,782 --> 00:20:15,185
In the films, he usually works for no
one, and that might be the biggest

305
00:20:15,235 --> 00:20:16,358
change of all.

306
00:20:16,408 --> 00:20:21,085
As Ridenour puts it, Hollywood tends
to turn him into an evil Santa.

307
00:20:25,027 --> 00:20:28,220
So let's go back to that
child in the farmhouse,

308
00:20:28,270 --> 00:20:30,742
staring at the basket in the doorway.

309
00:20:30,792 --> 00:20:33,181
What were they really looking at?

310
00:20:33,231 --> 00:20:38,902
Probably not a pagan god, and almost
certainly not the son of a Norse goddess.

311
00:20:38,952 --> 00:20:44,607
They were looking at a medieval stage
devil, chained to a saint who once

312
00:20:44,657 --> 00:20:49,574
pulled murdered children out of a
barrel, wearing the face of something

313
00:20:49,624 --> 00:20:53,650
much older that walked
the mountains in winter.

314
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:58,678
He was sold on postcards,
banned by a government,

315
00:20:58,728 --> 00:21:00,830
kept out of kindergartens…

316
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:04,370
and every single time, he came back.

317
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:11,290
If I had to choose, I'd say that's
the real secret of Krampus.

318
00:21:11,413 --> 00:21:16,999
He survives because he says out loud
what the rest of Christmas only

319
00:21:17,049 --> 00:21:22,090
whispers. Every gift
comes with a condition.

320
00:21:23,104 --> 00:21:25,086
So what do you think?

321
00:21:25,184 --> 00:21:30,562
Is Krampus a monster, or the
most honest part of Christmas?

322
00:21:30,612 --> 00:21:34,840
This is the very first file
we've opened on this channel,

323
00:21:34,890 --> 00:21:38,286
so let me know in the comments which
legend you'd like us to open next.

324
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:44,330
And Krampus wasn't the only thing that
came down from the mountains in winter.

325
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:49,683
In Iceland, they tell of a troll woman
who comes for misbehaving children

326
00:21:49,733 --> 00:21:53,726
at Christmas, carrying a sack of her own.

327
00:21:53,776 --> 00:21:56,410
That story is next.
